@baseUrl = http://localhost:8080
@apiVersion = 1.1

# ============================================================================
#  Eighth API Rules — sequential endpoint tester (VS Code REST Client)
# ============================================================================
#  Prerequisite: run the app first
#      cd eighth-api-rules/app && mvn spring-boot:run        (port 8080)
#
#  Run the requests in order, top to bottom. Comments mark expected responses,
#  and several requests reference the response of an earlier one (e.g. the
#  product created in Lesson 3). If you only execute a snippet, run its
#  dependencies first.
#
#  Law 7 note: API versioning is required, so almost every request carries the
#  "API-Version" header ({{apiVersion}} = 1.1). A request without it returns
#  400 — Lesson 7 demonstrates this on purpose.

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 1 — Design around resources, not actions
#  The URL names a resource (products, orders); the HTTP method names the
#  action. No /getProducts, /createOrder URLs anywhere.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 1a — GET the products collection (resource, not action)
###
### > Expected: 200 — { "content": [ ... 4 seeded products ], "page": 0, ... }
GET {{baseUrl}}/products
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 1b — GET one order by id (same pattern works for retrieve/replace/delete)
###
### > Expected: 200 — order 1, status "CREATED", single item "Clean Code"
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/1
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 1c — GET one product by id proves one URL serves many operations
###
### > Expected: 200 — product 1 "Clean Code" in the v1.1 shape
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/1
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 2 — Make URLs predictable
#  Always-plural nouns, camelCase JSON, nested sub-resources:
#  /orders/{id}/items is a predictable extension of /orders.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 2a — List all exposed product endpoints in one place
GET {{baseUrl}}/products
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 2b — Nested sub-resource: items of order 1
###
### > Expected: 200 — [ { "productId": 1, "productName": "Clean Code", ... } ]
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/1/items
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 2c — The orders collection uses the same plural-noun convention
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 3 — Use HTTP methods for their actual purpose
#  POST creates (201 + Location), PUT replaces, PATCH updates partially,
#  DELETE removes (204). POST is not idempotent; PUT/DELETE are.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 3a — POST creates a new product (NOT idempotent)
###
### > Expected: 201 + "Location: http://localhost:8080/products/5"
### > This response is reused below, so run it before the PUT/PATCH/DELETE tests.
# @name createProduct
POST {{baseUrl}}/products
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Mechanical Keyboard",
  "category": "electronics",
  "price": 89.00,
  "currency": "USD",
  "stock": 25
}

### Lesson 3b — Follow the Location header of the product just created
GET {{createProduct.response.headers.Location}}
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 3c — PUT replaces the whole resource (idempotent)
###
### > Expected: 200 — product now named "Mechanical Keyboard Pro"
PUT {{baseUrl}}/products/{{createProduct.response.body.$.id}}
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Mechanical Keyboard Pro",
  "category": "electronics",
  "price": 129.00,
  "currency": "USD",
  "stock": 25
}

### Lesson 3d — PATCH updates only the fields you send
###
### > Expected: 200 — price 99.00, name and stock untouched
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/products/{{createProduct.response.body.$.id}}
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "price": 99.00
}

### Lesson 3e — DELETE removes the product (idempotent, answers 204)
###
### > Expected: 204 No Content. Re-running this block will 404 the first
### > DELETE (already gone) — that itself is the "send it twice" lesson.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/products/{{createProduct.response.body.$.id}}
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 4 — Make status codes useful
#  Success: 200/201/204. Failures: 400, 404, 409, 422 — never "200 + error body".
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 4a — 404: the resource never existed
###
### > Expected: 404 — { "status": 404, "code": "PRODUCT_NOT_FOUND", ... }
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/999
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 4b — 422: request body fails validation
###
### > Expected: 422 — code "VALIDATION_FAILED" with fieldErrors for name/price/currency/stock
POST {{baseUrl}}/products
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "",
  "category": "office",
  "price": -1,
  "currency": "U",
  "stock": -5
}

### Lesson 4c — 409: conflict with the current state (insufficient stock)
###
### > Expected: 409 — code "INSUFFICIENT_STOCK". Desk Lamp (id 3) is seeded with 0 stock.
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Dave",
  "items": [
    {
      "productId": 3,
      "quantity": 1
    }
  ]
}

### Lesson 4d — 409: illegal status transition (order 1 is CREATED; CREATED → SHIPPED is not allowed)
###
### > Expected: 409 — code "INVALID_STATUS_TRANSITION"
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/orders/1/status
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "SHIPPED"
}

### Lesson 4e — 200: a legal transition (order 2 starts CONFIRMED; CONFIRMED → SHIPPED is allowed)
###
### > Expected: 200 — status "SHIPPED"
### > Note: re-running this block afterwards yields 409 "ORDER_ALREADY_IN_STATUS".
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/orders/2/status
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "SHIPPED"
}

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 5 — Keep error responses consistent
#  Every failure has the same six-field ApiError shape, signal to clients:
#  status, code, message, timestamp, fieldErrors.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 5a — Sample a 404 error container (machine + human fields)
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/999
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 5b — Sample a 422 with per-field errors (the 5th field carries detail)
POST {{baseUrl}}/products
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "",
  "category": "office",
  "price": -1,
  "currency": "U",
  "stock": -5
}

### Lesson 5c — 400: unknown enum value gets a consistent error body too
###
### > Expected: 400 — code "INVALID_STATUS"
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/orders/1/status
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "FROZEN"
}

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 6 — Don't put everything into the URL path
#  The path identifies the resource; query parameters refine the result;
#  HTTP methods describe the operation. No ?action=delete anywhere.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 6a — Filter by category (query parameter, not a path segment)
###
### > Expected: 200 — 2 "home" products (Desk Lamp, Tea Kettle)
GET {{baseUrl}}/products?category=home
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 6b — Filter by price range + availability
###
### > Expected: 200 — products priced 20–40 that are in stock
GET {{baseUrl}}/products?minPrice=20&maxPrice=40&inStock=true
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 6c — Sort and paginate
###
### > Expected: 200 — page 1 of size 2 ordered by price desc: [Desk Lamp, Digital Clock]
GET {{baseUrl}}/products?sort=price:desc&page=1&size=2
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 6d — Out-of-stock filter (Desk Lamp is the only seeded 0-stock item)
###
### > Expected: 200 — totalElements 1, the Desk Lamp
GET {{baseUrl}}/products?inStock=false
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 7 — Treat API changes carefully
#  v1.0 returns the original payload; v1.1 adds currency/stock additively.
#  The version comes from the API-Version header (see config/WebConfig.java).
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 7a — v1.0 contract (old shape: NO currency, NO stock)
###
### > Expected: 200 — { "id":1, "name":"Clean Code", ..., "price":39.99, "createdAt":"..." }
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/1
API-Version: 1.0

### Lesson 7b — v1.1 contract (additive: +currency, +stock; old fields unchanged)
### The diff between 7a and 7b is exactly the "additive, non-breaking change".
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/1
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 7c — No API-Version header is sent on purpose: 400 (version required)
###
### > Expected: 400 — Spring answers because versioning is enabled and required.
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/1

### Lesson 7d — Unsupported version is rejected: 400
###
### > Expected: 400 — 9.0 is not among the declared versions.
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/1
API-Version: 9.0

# ============================================================================
#  LESSON 8 — Keep request and response formats consistent
#  camelCase keys, ISO-8601 dates, one Pagination envelope, one error shape.
#  Note how every 200 collection below uses the exact same PageResponse JSON.
# ============================================================================

### Lesson 8a — The pagination envelope is identical across endpoints
GET {{baseUrl}}/products
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 8b — ...and the same envelope wraps orders
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 8c — Dates are ISO-8601 across the whole API (createdAt, timestamp)
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/1
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}

### Lesson 8d — And the error shape is identical everywhere (compare Lesson 5)
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/999
API-Version: {{apiVersion}}